<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumatralith</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sumatralith is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> made by flaking one face of a rounded river cobble around its edge, leaving the other side unworked. Made from quartzite or andesite by hunter-gatherers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> from the late Pleistocene to the middle Holocene, it is the tool most closely associated with the Hoabinhian, and archaeologists treat it as the chief sign of a Hoabinhian site. The name comes from North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, where J. Neumann first recovered such tools from a shell midden at Batu Kenong in 1920. Because so little of the cobble is shaped, a sumatralith could be resharpened again and again.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumatralith.jpg" alt="Sumatralith" width="330" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:临沧考古博物馆-旧石器时代-农克硝洞遗址-苏门答腊器1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sumatraliths are oval to rectangular tools, plano-convex in section: flat on one side and convex on the other. The blank is a quartzite or andesite cobble or pebble that already has roughly the flattened elliptical shape wanted, and the maker removed flakes by percussion only around its edge and only on one face to form the cutting edges. Much of the cobble's cortex remains on the finished tool. Because the shape is the same at every size, a worn tool could be resharpened and reshaped into a smaller one that still worked.</p><p>Early classifications divided them into three groups. Mühlhofer distinguished large broad, large narrow and small unifacial tools; Lebzelter separated oval, disc-shaped or elongated pick-like tools, flakes from pebbles and broad facetted flakes. Study of the Hoabinhian assemblages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> suggests two main uses, as scrapers and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-axe">hand axes</a>. The Hoabinhian assemblage excavated near Medan consisted almost entirely of sumatraliths, with a single broken biface.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Sumatraliths are found with animal and plant remains that point to everyday use in getting food. At Spirit Cave they lie beside hearths with evidence of peas, beans, almonds, betel, candlenuts, cucumbers and bottle gourds and of wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, pig and deer. Australian hunter-gatherers of modern times have been recorded hafting such tools as short axes on a handle at 45 degrees, and similar use is inferred for Hoabinhian groups.</p><p>Hoabinhian burials are rare, but some suggest a ritual role. A grave at Spirit Cave held its owner's sumatraliths, burials at Moh Khiew contained them, and a middle-aged man at Gua Gunung Runtuh was buried with one tool bearing wear from use in life. The 15 Hoabinhian burials at Gua Cha, however, have none.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Sumatraliths come from sites across mainland and parts of island <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, most often rock shelters and caves. The Hoabinhian's spread to South China or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> is debated, and the absence of sumatraliths there leads some archaeologists to call those sites Hoabinhian-like industries. Studies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> and Semang, modern hunter-gatherers thought to live in similar ways, suggest the makers ranged more widely than the surviving sites show.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sumatralith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Madeleine Colani sorted just 82 artefacts from Sao Dong into 28 types; her typology proved so complicated that most Hoabinhian sites are identified simply by the presence of <b>sumatraliths</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hoabinhian">Hoabinhian · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
